.TH SNAJPAGENT 1 "September 2026" "snajpagent" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
snajpagent \- fast, lightweight, networked coding agent
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snajpagent
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ \-\- ]
.RI [ initial-prompt " ...]"
.br
.B snajpagent \-\-resume
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ session-id " | --last]"
.RI "[-- " follow-up " ...]"
.br
.B snajpagent \-e
.RI [ options ]
.RI "[-- " prompt " ...]"
.br
.B snajpagent \-l
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B snajpagent
.RI [ options ]
.B login
.RI [ provider ]
.RI [ --device-auth " | --with-api-key]"
.br
.B snajpagent
.RI [ options ]
.B login status
.RI [ provider ]
.br
.B snajpagent
.RI [ options ]
.B logout
.RI [ provider ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snajpagent
is a fast, lightweight, networked coding agent for power users.  It runs in
the foreground, uses Responses-compatible model providers, executes coding
tools, and keeps resumable local sessions.  There is no resident service.
.PP
An interactive session can independently host one single-room IRC server and
maintain up to 16 outgoing
IRC connections.  Agents and channel operators share the room; the runtime
owns joining, history, and reconnection.  Startup flags and runtime slash
commands control these optional capabilities without changing session identity.
.SH GETTING STARTED
On a fresh interactive launch without configuration or existing environment
credentials, choose ChatGPT/Codex, OpenRouter, OpenAI, or a custom provider.
Complete device login or enter a masked API key (blank selects an environment
variable), optionally fetch that provider's catalog, and choose a model.
Setup creates a minimal private configuration and separate credential store,
then continues into the ordinary session.  Cancellation leaves prior settings
and credentials intact.
.PP
For manual configuration:
.PP
Create a private state directory:
.PP
.nf
install -d -m 700 "$HOME/.snajpagent"
.fi
.PP
Create
.IR $HOME/.snajpagent/config.ini ,
choosing a model supported by your provider:
.PP
.nf
[agent]
provider = openai
model = gpt-5.5

[provider openai]
base_url = https://api.openai.com
api_key = ${OPENAI_API_KEY}
.fi
.PP
Export the named credential, change to the project directory, and run:
.PP
.nf
export OPENAI_API_KEY='your-key'
cd /path/to/project
snajpagent
.fi
.PP
Enter submits a turn.  Type
.B /help
for commands and keys.
.SH BUILDING AND INSTALLING
Build from a Git checkout using GNU make, a C11/POSIX compiler with pthreads,
libcurl and Jansson development files.  Plain
.B make
builds only for the host, with size optimization and stripping.  ELF production
packaging needs strip and objcopy; macOS needs strip and dsymutil.
The build version uses the latest reachable Git tag; untagged commits add their
short revision, and modified checkouts add a -dirty suffix.
META defines the product name only.  A tagged Git history is required; portable
build targets pass the same derived identity into their isolated source build.
.TP
.B make DEBUG=1
Build unstripped debug code with frame pointers.  Both profiles keep the same
features and runtime checks.  Switching profiles does not require cleaning;
do not run different profiles concurrently in one worktree.
.TP
.B make install
Build and install production under
.IR /usr/local ,
with executable mode 0755 and manual mode 0644.  Use
.B make DEBUG=1 install
for a deliberate debug installation.
.B PREFIX
changes the prefix;
.B MANPREFIX
defaults to $(PREFIX)/share/man.
.B DESTDIR
stages both paths without installing on the host.
.TP
.B make help
List targets and overrides without building or contacting a provider.
.TP
.B make check
Run existing local tests and source checks.  Tests need Python 3 and Perl;
tmux enables the terminal integration tests.
.PP
Explicit CC, CPPFLAGS, CFLAGS and LDFLAGS replace their profile defaults;
required identity and thread flags remain.  Production defaults to
.B -Os
and debug to
.BR -Og .
Neither default adds host-specific instruction sets or mandatory LTO.
STRIP, OBJCOPY and DSYMUTIL override packaging tools.
.PP
Production retains matching symbols beside the build executable:
.I debug-snajpagent
on ELF or
.I snajpagent.dSYM
on macOS.  They are not installed or needed to run the program.  Keep them
for diagnosing that exact binary; a later debug rebuild is not a substitute.
.SS Standalone builds
These explicit targets use the pinned Nix recipes and can download/build
dependencies.  They do not replace the normal host executable or start VMs.
Independent targets can run together with
.BR "make -jN" .
.TP
.BR "make prod-linux-x86_64" , " make prod-linux-aarch64"
Build a static-PIE Linux executable with embedded CA data.  Copy
.I build/matrix/linux-ARCH/bin/snajpagent
to the destination; Nix and third-party runtime libraries are not required
there.  Optional matching symbols are in the adjacent
.I .debug/
directory.  These target modern Linux with x86-64 or ARMv8-A baselines;
older-kernel compatibility requires separate qualification.
.TP
.BR "make prod-macos-arm64" , " make prod-macos-x86_64"
Cross-build a macOS 11+ executable with static application libraries and CA
data; only the OS libSystem remains dynamic.  Output is
.IR build/matrix/macos-ARCH/bin/snajpagent ,
with an adjacent matching dSYM.
.TP
.B make prod-macos-universal
Combine both Mac slices into a native Mach-O universal executable and dSYM
under
.IR build/matrix/macos-universal/bin/ .
.PP
The macOS cross-builds are experimental: cross-built and inspected, not yet
runtime-qualified on macOS.  No Developer ID signing or notarization is implied.
Windows currently has dependency/platform foundations, not a complete agent
or a public production target.  Cross-compilation alone does not establish
runtime support.
.PP
See the repository's DEPENDENCIES.md for static dependency licenses, embedded
trust, corresponding-source obligations and platform qualification details.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s " [" endpoint "]", " \-\-listen" [= endpoint ]
Host the built-in IRC server in this foreground process and select its
listener.  This does not detach.  A missing endpoint means
.BR localhost:6667 .
An address already in use is a startup error, visible without verbosity flags.
It does not try another address or increment the port after that collision.
.TP
.BR \-c " [" endpoint "]", " \-\-client" [= endpoint ]
Add an outgoing IRC connection.  Repeat for up to 16 distinct endpoints.  A
missing endpoint means
.BR localhost:6667 .
.TP
.BR \-\-no-listen , " \-\-no-client"
Independently suppress a configured listener or clear configured outgoing
connections.  Each conflicts with its positive startup flag; both negatives
may be used together.  Nick and room preferences remain usable while offline.
.TP
.BI \-n " nick, " \-\-model-nick " nick"
Set the model's preferred IRC nick.  Default:
.BR agent0 .
.TP
.BI \-o " nick, " \-\-operator-nick " nick"
Set the local operator's IRC nick.  The default is a valid
.B USER
value plus
.BR 0 ,
then
.BR operator0 ,
or
.B localop0
when needed to remain distinct from the model nick.
.TP
.BI \-r " room, " \-\-room-name " room"
Set the preferred room for current or future hosting.
A leading
.B #
is optional.  The default is derived from the hostname.
.TP
.BI \-C " directory"
Use an existing directory as the workspace.  With
.BR \-\-resume ,
relocate the resumed session to it.
.TP
.BI \-d " directory"
Advertise an additional local working-document root.  Repeat for multiple roots.
Each directory must contain a regular, non-symlink
.B AGENTS.md
or
.BR AGENTS.override.md ;
the override takes precedence.  Relative paths resolve against the launch
directory, independently of
.BR \-C .
Canonical duplicate paths are collapsed.  These roots are invocation-local;
the printed resume command includes them.  They remain explicit pointers even
when automatic discovery is disabled.  They do not grant unrelated write
permission or synchronize documents between hosts.
.TP
.BI \-m " model"
Override the next model.  On resume this is a one-turn override unless combined
with an explicit provider selection or selected again in the session.
.TP
.BI \-\-effort " level"
Override reasoning effort.  On resume this is a one-turn override unless
combined with an explicit provider selection.
.TP
.BI \-\-dotdir " directory"
Use an absolute private application directory instead of
.IR $HOME/.snajpagent .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Read an absolute configuration path.  This does not move sessions or the model
cache.  Unlike the default path, an explicit file must exist.
.TP
.BI \-\-provider " name"
Select a configured provider. On resume this explicitly changes the saved
selection after local recovery, without rewriting past events. A missing name
is an error. Combine with -m MODEL when changing models too.
.TP
.BR \-v
Set verbosity to the number of flags, starting at 0 without flags.
More than six flags is an error.  The runtime command
.B /verbose N
sets the same process-local level.  See
.BR DISPLAY .
.TP
.BR \-\-color [= when ]
Set color to
.BR auto ,
.BR always ,
or
.BR never .
Bare
.B \-\-color
means
.BR always .
The value may follow a space or
.BR = .
.TP
.BR \-\-no-color
Equivalent to
.BR \-\-color=never .
.TP
.BR \-\-markdown
Render supported model Markdown on terminals.  This is the default.
.TP
.BR \-\-no-markdown
Show model Markdown literally.
.TP
.BR \-\-resume " [" session-id "]"
Resume a durable session.  Without an ID or
.BR \-\-last ,
interactively select an active session in the current workspace.
An ID is a unique prefix of 8 through 32 lowercase hexadecimal characters.
An exact ID may select an archived session; a live session lock prevents
concurrent resume.
.TP
.BR \-\-last
Resume the most recently used matching active session.  Requires
.BR \-\-resume .
.TP
.BR \-\-all
Include other workspaces.  With
.BR \-l ,
also include archived sessions.  With
.BR \-\-resume ,
it applies to selection and cannot accompany an exact ID.
.TP
.BR \-e
Execute one turn and exit.  The prompt must follow
.B \-\-
or arrive on non-terminal standard input.  Network options are not accepted.
.TP
.BR \-l
List sessions and exit.  Only
.BR \-\-all ,
.BR \-\-dotdir ,
.BR \-\-config ,
color options, and Markdown presentation options may accompany it.  Markdown
presentation has no useful effect on session-list output.
.TP
.BR \-h
Print short help and exit.  It must stand alone.
.TP
.BR \-V
Print build identity and exit.  It must stand alone.
.PP
An endpoint is
.IR host ,
.IR host:port ,
.IR [IPv6] ,
or
.IR [IPv6]:port .
The default port is 6667.  In networked mode initial chat text must follow
.BR \-\- .
.SH INTERACTIVE INPUT
.TP
.B Enter
Submit while idle.  In rollout during model generation, interrupt the response
and steer from its delivered prefix.  A running command is handed back alive;
it is not killed.  In chat, send to the selected room.  A model-nick mention
steers at the next safe response/tool boundary without truncating generation.
.TP
.B Tab
Complete a slash command or numeric IRC destination when possible.  In chat, complete an
.B @nick
at the cursor from current room members.  Ambiguous matches expand their common
prefix; a second consecutive Tab lists the matching choices and restores the
draft and cursor.  Unique matches include a trailing space, reusing an existing
space before retained text.  These rules apply to every completion type.
No match leaves the draft unchanged.  Completion never queues or sends text.
While idle, otherwise insert spaces.
During a turn, queue the draft for a later FIFO turn, except that
.B /verbose
applies locally immediately.  Empty Tab always switches
chat and rollout views, including offline.
.TP
.B Ctrl-J
Insert a newline without submitting.
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
Leave the visible draft in scrollback, append literal
.BR ^C ,
start a new line, and open a clean prompt.  With an empty active composer, also
interrupt the turn.  Five consecutive presses within two seconds exit;
other input resets the sequence.  Ctrl-D on an empty draft or
.B /exit
while idle also exits.
.TP
.B Ctrl-D
Delete at the cursor when text remains.  On an empty draft, interrupt active
work and exit without waiting for the model or command to finish.
.TP
.BR Up , " Down"
Move through the shared persistent prompt history while preserving the current
draft at the newest boundary.
.TP
.B Ctrl-R
Start case-sensitive incremental reverse search over prompt history.  Press it
again for the next older match; search does not wrap.
.TP
.B Ctrl-G
Abort reverse search and restore the exact original draft and cursor.  Escape
accepts the current match without submitting it; editing and movement keys
accept it before applying their normal action.
.TP
.BR Left , " Right" , " Home" , " End" , " Delete" , " Backspace"
Edit the UTF-8 draft.
.TP
.B Ctrl-U
Clear the draft.
.TP
.B Ctrl-W
Delete the preceding word.
.TP
.B Ctrl-L
Clear and redraw the terminal.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Z
Suspend after restoring normal terminal state; redraw after continuation.
.PP
During output, editing opens a live composer without stopping the provider.
Visible output resumes after
.B typing_pause_ms
of inactivity.  Bracketed paste inserts pasted newlines without submitting.
.PP
Direct prompts accept up to 1 MiB of valid UTF-8; steering and queued turns
accept up to 256 KiB.  The queue holds up to 128 pending turns within 16 MiB;
up to 32 steering items may be pending in a turn.  Interactive EOF interrupts
active work, pauses further queued or goal work, and exits.  The session remains
resumable.
.SH DISPLAY
Startup without network roles opens rollout; startup with roles opens chat. Empty Tab,
.BR /chat ,
and
.B /rollout
switch views at idle or during work, including offline.  Runtime networking
changes preserve view, draft, history and verbosity.  Offline chat retains its
transcript; Enter rejects unsendable input and restores the draft, without
starting a private turn. Hidden-view output is buffered for display on return.
Both views append to terminal scrollback;
neither is a full-screen TUI.
.PP
The default rollout prompt starts with a goal slot and a shared activity slot,
then a bare context percentage space-padded to four columns, a space,
provider/model/effort, and a space before its idle or active marker:
.B ›
while idle and
.B »
while active.  The percentage uses the latest comparable token bound and
resolved input budget, rounded up.  A fresh or incomparable state shows
.BR 0% ;
an unknown budget shows
.BR ?% .
.PP
The default chat prompt is
.IR "   HH:MM:SS " OPERATOR_NICK@MACHINE_HOSTNAME " : "
when idle.  Its first two spaces reserve the indicator slots; one literal
space separates them from the clock in every activity state.
The timestamp is local time captured once per composer.  Editing, view changes,
and status redraws retain it; submission or Ctrl-C cancellation ends that capture.
The goal flag is independent of the activity slot.  Tool calls take priority
over model/provider work in the same activity column; idle reserves one space.
Unused context digits also remain spaces.  Interruption retains the current provider/tool indicator until the
turn closes; there is no separate interruption glyph.  The template and spinners are configurable under
.BR [ui] .
.SS Verbosity
Verbosity controls local rollout and diagnostics, not chat-message visibility
or which view is selected.  All participants see the same room messages and
retained history at every level, including their own sends.
Each message carries up to 4096 UTF-8 bytes (at least 1024 Unicode code points).
Longer text splits at word boundaries when possible.  The extended IRC wire
limit is 8192 bytes including CRLF, advertised as LINELEN=8192;
peers must support that extension for long messages.
.TP
.B 0
Model text in the rollout; timestamped room traffic in chat.  Rollout milestones
include the colored bullet lines
.BR "• Compacted" ,
.BR "• Goal set" ,
and
.BR "• Goal cleared" .
Errors and local-command results appear in either view.
.TP
.B 1
Compact tool start and outcome rows, with a short generic argument prefix.
No output body.
.TP
.B 2
Add previews of up to 1024 argument and 512 output characters per invocation.
Count terminal-safe Unicode code
points; mark omitted content explicitly.
.TP
.B 3
Full retained tool arguments, effective execution context and results, subject
to existing capture limits and
.BR "[tool] max_output_bytes" .
.TP
.B 4
Add runtime decisions, token accounting, synced event identities and connection
diagnostics.  Session identity and explicit command replies are always complete.
.TP
.B 5
Add sanitized provider protocol and IRC commands.  This exposes prompts,
source, tool output, and model content.
.TP
.B 6
Add transport diagnostics.
.PP
Levels 4 through 6 are live diagnostics in visible rollout; they are not queued
behind chat.  Unseen retained work uses the current level on entry to rollout.
Increasing the level does not replay visited history; lowering it stops now-hidden
detail between bounded slices.  Redaction of known credentials is not a guarantee
that source, prompts or tool output contain no secrets.
.SS Presentation
.PP
Terminal model text uses color and compact Markdown presentation when enabled.
Headings, lists, quotes, fenced code, pipe tables, inline code, emphasis,
strikethrough, and links are supported.  Pipe tables align as a grid when they
fit and become labeled rows on narrow terminals.  Redirected and durable text
retains the model's exact bytes.
.PP
Top-level prose paragraphs are bulleted.  Prompts and model blocks have one
empty row between them; generated prose wraps align below the paragraph text.
Timestamped IRC chat messages do not receive the synthetic paragraph bullet;
genuine Markdown list items retain their structural bullets.
.SH COMMANDS
Commands normally occupy a single submitted line.
.BR /ro ,
numbered sends and
.B /all
also accept multiline message text.  Prefix text with
.B //
to submit a literal leading
.BR / .
.SS Session and model
.TP
.BR /help , " /?"
Show commands and essential keys.
.TP
.B /status
Show session, selection, queue, verbosity, capacity, token accounting, requested
network roles and actual connection state.
.TP
.B /history
Show the most recent retained user and assistant exchange.
.TP
.BR /model " [list]"
Read the local provider/model catalog.  No provider request is made.
.TP
.B /model cache
Refresh every configured provider through its authenticated model endpoint,
replace the cache atomically, and display it.  A normalized API path ending in
.B /backend-api/codex
uses
.BR /models?client_version=0.146.0 ;
other providers use
.BR /v1/models .
There is no automatic catalog refresh or protocol fallback.  Failure leaves
the previous cache intact.  Idle only.  See
.BR "MODEL SELECTION AND CONTEXT" .
.TP
.BR /model " " number ", " /model " #" number
Select an exact displayed provider/model/effort row.
.TP
.BI /model " selector" " [save|s]"
Select
.IR model ,
.IR "model / effort" ,
or
.IR "provider / model / effort" .
A final
.B save
or
.B s
writes the selection to the active configuration.  Typed model IDs need not
exist in the cache.  Use a numbered row for an ID containing
.BR / .
Selection is idle only.  The final save suffix also works with numbered rows.
.TP
.B /config
Open the active configuration with
.BR EDITOR .
Valid changes are reloaded; invalid content remains on disk
while the prior runtime configuration stays active.  Only IRC components changed
since the last successful file load override current process state.  An unrelated
edit preserves runtime additions/removals; editing the client list replaces that
list.  Startup networking flags are not reapplied over deliberately edited fields.
Only affected owners restart.  Failed restoration reports actual remaining roles.
Reload preserves the process's current verbosity and view.  Idle only.
.TP
.BR /effort " [" level "]"
Show or set reasoning effort.  Setting is idle only.
.TP
.BR /verbose " [0..6]"
Show or set this process's current verbosity immediately, even during active
work (Enter or active-turn Tab).  Subsequent output uses the new level;
already visited detail is not replayed.  It is local feedback, never chat or
model input.  The printed resume
command carries this current level, not the original startup flag count.
.TP
.B /archive
Archive the idle session and exit.  Exact resume can unarchive it.
.TP
.B /compact
Compact idle model context.  The full local event log is retained.
.TP
.B /delete
Confirm with the displayed session prefix, then permanently remove the idle
session through private trash staging.  Shared prompt history is not erased.
.TP
.B /exit
Preserve the session and exit.  Idle only.
.SS Read-only queries
.TP
.BI /ro " query"
Run one read-only query, including multiline text.  Native tools list
files/directories, read whole files or inclusive 1-based line ranges, and
search text patterns without shells or external commands.  Provider-hosted
web search is also available when supported by the selected provider/model;
search queries are sent to that provider.  Normal capabilities return on the
next ordinary
turn.  In networked mode the query opens rollout view and sends no IRC reply.
During active work, Enter rejects this mode switch and preserves the draft;
press Tab or use
.B /queue /ro query
to run it after the current turn.  Ordinary steering inside a read-only turn
cannot lift its restrictions.  Empty queries are rejected;
.B //ro
is literal text.  One-shot argument and stdin prompts also support this prefix.
.TP
.B /retry
After a turn fails, continue it from retained conversation and tool results.
The original prompt is not duplicated and completed tools are not replayed.
Read-only mode is preserved; current next-turn model settings apply.
Available in both views and after session resume, until another turn starts.
Active turns cannot be retried. Queued work and paused goals stay paused.
Turn failures display a hint to try this command.
.SS Goals
An active goal continues across ordinary turns until complete, paused,
cancelled, or blocked.  Queued operator turns run before the next automatic
goal turn.  Failures, refusal and turn-only interruption pause automatic
continuation.  Quitting the foreground process interrupts its turn but does
not pause its goal.  Session resume preserves the saved goal state unchanged.
.TP
.BR /goal , " /goal status"
Show the durable goal.  Queued prompts run before automatic goal continuation,
without fresh goal reminders throughout those turns.  A retained unarmed queue
cannot be bypassed: use
.B /next
or explicit goal start/resume to arm it.  An open queue editor prevents draining.
.TP
.BI /goal " text"
Start a goal, or reword the unfinished goal.  Quote the text or use
.B /goal set
when its first word is a goal command.
.TP
.BI /goal " set text"
Explicitly start or reword a goal.
.TP
.B /goal pause
Pause automatic continuation at the current turn boundary.
.TP
.B /goal resume
Resume a paused or blocked goal.
.TP
.BR /goal " lock|unlock"
Deny or permit model-authored wording changes.  Operator rewording remains
available.
.TP
.BR /goal " complete|cancel"
End the current goal from the operator side.
.TP
.B /goal help
Show the complete goal grammar.
.SS Queue
.TP
.BR /queue , " /q"
List future FIFO turns.
.TP
.BI /queue " text"
Queue text during an active turn.  Tab is the direct equivalent for a draft.
.TP
.BR /queue " clear|pop, " /q " c|p"
Remove all queued turns or the newest one.
.TP
.BI /queue " n delete, " /q " n" d
Delete queued item
.IR n .
.TP
.BI /queue " n edit, " /q " n" e
Reopen queued item
.I n
in the composer.
.TP
.B /next
Run the oldest queued turn after resume or another paused state.  Idle only.
.SS Network
All network commands are discoverable and usable at idle and during provider
streaming, silence, retries, token counting and running tools.  They are local
controls, not model input, steering, cancellation or future turns.  Endpoints use
the same syntax as startup flags; an omitted endpoint defaults to localhost:6667.
.TP
.BR /server , " /server start " [ENDPOINT], " /server stop"
Report hosting, start it, or stop only the hosted listener and accepted peers.
Outgoing connections remain unchanged.  Repeating the current listener is a
no-op; replacing it requires an explicit stop first.  Bind failure leaves the
session and unrelated endpoints usable.  Hosting publishes only matching public
room history, never private rollout or another room's history.
.TP
.BR /connect " " [ENDPOINT]
Add an outgoing connection, up to 16.  A duplicate is a no-op, including while
reconnecting.  Connecting is asynchronous and is not reported as a completed join.
.TP
.BR /disconnect " " [ENDPOINT]
Remove the named outgoing endpoint, or all outgoing endpoints when omitted.
Hosting is unchanged.  Stop reconnect attempts and report discarded unsent bytes;
do not transfer them to another endpoint.  Already accepted model input remains
eligible after final disconnection.  Old model sends are not redirected into
replacement rooms; they receive normal not-performed tool results.
.TP
.B /chat
Select the public chat transcript, including when offline.
.TP
.B /rollout
Select local model activity.
.TP
.BR /topic " [" text "]"
Show the IRC snapshot, or change the topic in the selected room where the local
operator has op status.  Text is at most 280 bytes, without newlines.
.TP
.B /names
Show numbered destinations, selection, endpoints, members, and channel-operator flags.
.TP
.BR /N " [" text "]"
Select numbered destination N.  With text, send there once without changing
selection.  Numbers remain stable for this process and are not reused after
removal.  With a sole destination the same syntax remains valid.
.TP
.B /all text
Broadcast once to the destinations present at submission.  This does not
enable a persistent broadcast mode.  A later connection does not receive it.
An unavailable destination is reported locally; messages are not redirected.
.SH MODEL SELECTION AND CONTEXT
The provider, model, and effort are session preferences.  A typed selector
without a provider stays on the currently selected provider.  An omitted
effort uses configuration.  The value
.B default
for the model resolves to the compiled default;
.B default
for effort resolves to
.BR medium .
Other nonblank bounded values are sent to the provider without checking a
model catalog.
.PP
Use
.B /model cache
to discover models and advertised capacities.  Listing the cache is offline;
there is no TTL and no dependency on an installed Codex.  Cached limits and
learned accounting apply only to their original provider, URL, protocol, and
model.  An explicit
.B [model-limit PROVIDER/MODEL]
overrides advertised capacities.  Missing capacities remain unknown.
.PP
Token accounting prefers exact provider preflight when supported, then uses
completed request usage, source-bound observations, or conservative byte
bounds.  These methods are not interchangeable exact counts.
.B /status
shows the capacity source and accounting method.  Catalog refresh resets the
count-capability probe and preserves observations only for an identical source.
.PP
Automatic compaction uses the provider threshold and resolved input capacity;
setting the proactive threshold to zero does not disable capacity handling.
Native compaction is used when enabled.  Set
.B native_compaction=false
to summarize through ordinary Responses.  Direct ChatGPT/Codex also falls back
once to Responses summarization when its native endpoint is unavailable;
other native failures remain errors.  Manual
.B /compact
is idle only.  Compaction changes model context, not the append-only local log.
.SH LOGIN
Common options such as
.BR --dotdir ,
.BR --config ,
and
.B -m
must precede the login/logout command.
.TP
.BI "login " [provider]
Authenticate a configured provider or choose one of the
.BR codex ,
.BR openrouter ,
.BR openai ,
and
.B custom
presets.  Existing environment authentication remains explicit; there is no
fallback to another provider or authentication method.  Login does not change
the default model in an existing configuration.
.TP
.B --device-auth
Use the native Codex device flow.  Open the printed verification URL and enter
the one-time code.  Device login must be enabled in the ChatGPT account or
workspace.  Waiting is bounded to 15 minutes; Ctrl-C cancels.
.TP
.B --with-api-key
Read one API key from stdin for explicit login.  No key argument is accepted.
Noninteractive first setup also requires
.B -m MODEL
before
.BR login .
.TP
.BI "login status " [provider]
Report configured credential sources and local state without contacting a
provider or refreshing tokens.  Stored status is not a remote entitlement check.
.TP
.BI "logout " [provider]
Remove only the selected provider's stored login.  If omitted, select the
configured default provider.  Environment variables and Codex's own credentials
are not modified; other provider logins remain intact.
.PP
Automatic setup never consumes piped task input or runs for execution, resume,
session listing, or a missing explicitly specified configuration.  Invalid
existing configuration is an error, not an invitation to overwrite it.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The file is UTF-8 INI syntax.  Lines beginning with
.B #
or
.B ;
are comments; there are no inline comments or general shell-style quotes.
Unknown sections, keys, duplicate scalar keys, invalid values, and files over
64 KiB are errors.  Booleans are
.B true
or
.BR false .
The numeric forms 1 and 0 are also accepted for boolean settings.
Command-line values override file values.  A missing default configuration
uses built-ins; an explicit file must exist.  Configuration must be a regular
file, not a symlink.  Section names below go in square brackets.
.SS agent
.TP
.B provider
Provider for new sessions.  Default: first configured provider.
.TP
.B model
Model for new sessions.  Default:
.BR default ,
which resolves to
.BR gpt-5.5-2026-04-23 .
.TP
.B reasoning_effort
Default:
.B default
(resolves to
.BR medium ).
The value is provider-specific; common choices include
.BR low ,
.BR medium ,
and
.BR high .
.TP
.B max_goal_prompt_bytes
Goal wording limit, 1 through 1048576.  Default: 262144.
.TP
.B read_agents_md
Discover global and project
.B AGENTS.override.md
or
.B AGENTS.md
instruction-file paths before each turn.  Default:
.BR true .
The model chooses relevant files to read with tools; contents are not injected.
Explicit
.B \-d
roots are independent of this setting.  See
.BR FILES .
.SS "provider NAME"
Up to 16 ordered named sections are accepted.  The spelling
.B [provider]
is not accepted. Names contain 1 through 63 ASCII letters, digits, dots,
underscores or hyphens. Names have no privileged meaning. New sessions use the
first declaration unless an explicit provider preference or
.B --provider NAME
selects another. Unqualified model changes stay on the current provider.
.TP
.B base_url
HTTP or HTTPS API base without query or fragment.  Default:
.BR https://api.openai.com .
.TP
.B api_key
Credential source: ${ENV_NAME}, a double-quoted literal with JSON escaping,
or an unquoted file path. A bare environment name means a file, not a variable.
Relative files use the config directory; leading ~/ expands the user's home.
Files may have one final LF/CRLF and may be symlinks for rotation.
Literal secrets require a user-owned configuration with no group/other
permissions; mode 0600 is the normal choice.
Absent means use the provider's managed stored login; empty is invalid.
.TP
.B auth
Authentication method:
.B api_key
(default) uses the explicit source or, when absent, the provider's stored key; and
.B chatgpt
uses stored Codex OAuth credentials with automatic refresh.
ChatGPT auth requires the exact base
.BR https://chatgpt.com/backend-api/codex
and no api_key setting.
These tokens are never sent to a custom endpoint.  Existing bearer-auth
Codex-compatible proxies use
.BR auth=api_key .
Missing/bad explicit sources never fall back to a stored login.
.TP
.B connect_timeout_ms
Connect timeout, 1000 through 120000.  Default: 30000.
.TP
.B idle_timeout_ms
No-data timeout, 1000 through 600000.  Default: 120000.
.TP
.B request_timeout_ms
Whole-request timeout, 1000 through 3600000.  Default: 1800000.
.TP
.B auto_compact_input_tokens
Proactive input threshold.  Default:
.BR auto .
Auto uses 90% of the selected model's effective hard input budget, rounded
down with a minimum of one token, after output reservation and safety limits.
It follows model/context changes and learned lower ceilings.  If capacity is
unknown, it uses a labelled 120000-token fallback.
An integer from 1 through 4000000 selects a fixed threshold; zero disables
proactive policy, not the hard capacity guard.  Existing explicit numbers
remain fixed: remove the setting or use
.B auto
to enable scaling.
.B /model
and
.B /status
show the effective threshold and its mode without changing configuration.
.TP
.B exact_token_count
Input-token preflight policy.
.B auto
prefers the provider's Responses count endpoint and falls back only when it is
definitively unsupported;
.B true
requires exact preflight; and
.B false
disables it.  Default:
.BR auto .
OpenRouter's absent count endpoint (HTTP 404) uses the automatic fallback;
strict counting still fails.  Other providers' ambiguous 404 responses and
authentication failures do not disable exact counting.
.TP
.B native_compaction
Use the provider's native compaction endpoint.  When false, use an ordinary
Responses summarization request instead.  Default:
.BR true .
.PP
For direct ChatGPT/Codex, an unavailable native compaction endpoint falls back
once to Responses summarization. Authentication failures do not trigger this
fallback, and the configured provider/account and hard context guard stay intact.
.TP
.B parallel_tool_calls
Request native multiple function calls from this provider.  Default: true.
Set false for a provider/model that cannot batch calls.  This does not disable
local yielded-command concurrency or change provider/account selection.
.TP
.B openrouter_referer
Optional printable ASCII
.B HTTP-Referer
value.
.TP
.B openrouter_title
Optional printable ASCII
.B X-Title
value.
.SS "model-limit PROVIDER/MODEL"
Up to 128 rules are accepted.
Set provider-wide defaults with [model-limit PROVIDER], patterned rules with
[model-limit PROVIDER/PATTERN], and exact rules with [model-limit PROVIDER/MODEL].
The first slash separates the provider; remaining slashes belong to the model.
Set at least one of:
.TP
.B context_window_tokens
Total context tokens, 1 through 4000000000.
.TP
.B max_input_tokens
Maximum input tokens, 1 through 4000000000.
.TP
.B max_output_tokens
Maximum output tokens, 1 through 4000000000.
.PP
Rules merge per field: provider-wide, matching patterns in file order, then
exact model. The star matches zero or more characters over the whole local
model name. Duplicate targets/keys are errors. Unspecified fields inherit
catalog metadata. Explicit values supersede the catalog, but output headroom
and learned backend ceilings still constrain usable input.
.SS "model-alias PROVIDER/NAME"
Up to 128 declarations are accepted. The local name uses the provider-name
syntax. The required model key is a literal upstream model ID. These declarations
define ordinary provider-local models with independent limits; several names
may route to the same upstream model. Targets never resolve recursively.
Selection, sessions and limits use the local name; provider requests use the
upstream ID. Upstream names do not add another limit-rule matching tier.
.PP
For example, give one provider-local model a smaller context budget:
.PP
.nf
[model-alias openai/review]
model = gpt-5.5

[model-limit openai]
max_output_tokens = 16000

[model-limit openai/review]
context_window_tokens = 128000
.fi
.PP
Select it with
.B --provider openai -m review
or
.BR "/model openai/review/high" .
.SS ui
.TP
.B color
.BR auto ,
.BR always ,
or
.BR never .
Default:
.BR auto .
.TP
.B markdown
Render supported terminal Markdown.  Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.B resume_history_turns
Resume-history switch, parsed from 0 through 100.  Zero disables display; any
nonzero value shows the single most recent retained user and assistant
exchange on a non-networked resume.  Default: 2.  Network chat uses actual
room history instead of privately generated assistant text.
.TP
.B typing_pause_ms
Output pause after an input edit, 0 through 5000.  Default: 500.
.TP
.B prompt
Data-only prompt template containing exactly one
.BR {chat:TEXT} ,
.BR {rollout-idle:TEXT} ,
and
.BR {rollout-active:TEXT}
case.  Ordinary fields are
.BR {provider} ,
.BR {model} ,
.BR {effort} ,
.BR {operator} ,
.BR {host} ,
.BR {context} ,
.BR {mode} ,
.BR {queue} ,
.BR {hour} ,
.BR {minute} ,
and
.BR {second} ;
optional status fields are
.BR {goal_spinner}
and
.BR {activity_spinner} .
The old separate provider/tool placeholders are removed pre-1.0; explicit
templates must replace them with one shared activity placeholder.
Backslash escapes literal braces and backslash.  No shell, environment, or
command expansion occurs.  snajpagent appends one space after the selected
label.
.PP
.B {queue}
expands to the number of pending future prompts in parentheses followed by one
space, such as "(1) ", or nothing when the queue is empty.  Default rollout
prompts place it immediately before the idle/active marker; default chat and
queue-edit prompts are unchanged.  It includes paused prompts, excludes the
running turn, and updates as prompts are queued, started or removed.
.PP
Numeric fields accept a minimum space-padded width, for example
.B {context:4}
renders the complete percentage as "  0%", "  9%", " 10%", "100%", or "  ?%".
Clock components are natural decimal digits and additionally accept zero-fill:
.B {hour:02}:{minute:02}:{second:02}
is the default local clock.  Bare fields are unpadded.  Failed time capture
renders each component as "--", padded with spaces even for a zero-fill format.
Widths never truncate, include the percent sign, and must be positive decimal
integers at most 510.  The complete label, input space, and largest spinner
frames must still fit 512 bytes including NUL.  Signs, whitespace, extra leading
zeros, empty widths, and formats on text/spinner fields are rejected in every
mode.  Context zero-fill is invalid.  Invalid reloads retain the previous config.
The pre-1.0 combined
.B {time}
field is removed: manually replace it in explicit templates with clock components.
.PP
For a minimal prompt without spinners, use this single-line value:
.nf
prompt = {chat:{operator}:}{rollout-idle:›}{rollout-active:»}
.fi
.PP
Templates must be printable valid UTF-8, at most 1024 bytes.  All three cases
are required and nonempty; text outside them is shared.  A case may omit fields.
Spinner fields may each occur at most once per case.  The default appearance
is described under
.BR DISPLAY .
.TP
.BR prompt_spinner_goal , " prompt_spinner_provider" , " prompt_spinner_tool"
Quoted inactive-item plus active-frame strings.  The first safe one-column
Unicode item is the inactive rendering.  Exact leading file characters
.B \e0
instead select zero-width inactive rendering; no NUL byte is stored.  A space
therefore reserves a stable column, while
.B \(dq\e0\(dq
removes a present field and
.B \(dq\e0⚑\(dq
appears only while active.  Zero through 16 remaining one-column items are
active frames.  One active frame is static and schedules no periodic refresh;
two or more animate.  Adjacent active frames must differ.  A blank active
frame still occupies one column.  Omit a placeholder to remove it in one mode.
Literal spaces are never trimmed, and numeric padding never restores an absent
spinner.  The activity slot uses the tool sequence during a tool call,
otherwise the provider sequence (its inactive item when idle).  A selected
sequence with no active frames uses its inactive item.  Defaults:
.PP
.nf
prompt_spinner_goal = " ⚑"
prompt_spinner_provider = " ◴◷◶◵"
prompt_spinner_tool = " ⠋⠙⠹⠸⠼⠴⠦⠧⠇⠏"
.fi
.TP
.B prompt_spinner_per_second
Shared animation rate, 1 through 60.  Default: 8.  Hidden and static indicators
do not animate.
.SS tool
.TP
.B shell
Absolute executable used by
.BR exec_command .
Default:
.BR /bin/sh .
.TP
.B default_yield_ms
Default initial yield interval, 0 through 600000.  Default: 10000.
.TP
.B max_parallel_commands
Maximum unsettled command handles, including yielded and ready-but-uncollected
jobs.  Range: 1 through 32.  Default: 4.  One runs commands serially without
restoring the former tool-catalog lock.  This is not a subprocess or CPU limit.
.TP
.B default_timeout_ms
Foreground handoff deadline when the model supplies none, 0 through 4294967295.
Zero means no deadline.  Must not exceed
.BR max_timeout_ms .
Default: 0.  Reaching the deadline does not kill the command.
.TP
.B max_timeout_ms
Maximum positive model deadline, 1 through 4294967295.  Default: 86400000.
.TP
.B max_output_tokens
Command-result context ceiling, 1 through 4000000000.  Default: 6000.
A tool call selects a smaller positive bound or
.B null
for this ceiling.  Larger requests are capped at this value.
.TP
.B max_output_bytes
Maximum result bytes displayed per tool call, 0 through 4294967295.  Zero
means unlimited.  The
durable session retains complete redacted output; model context has its own
tool-call bound.  Default: 0.
.TP
.B secret
Repeatable additional value to protect, using the same source syntax as api_key.
Up to 64 sources. Environment references are removed from child environments;
files/literals are not exported. Missing required sources fail before protected
work. The obsolete secret_env key is rejected.
.SS irc
.TP
.B listen
Enable the server and set its listener.  Absent by default.  A bare command-line
.BR \-s
uses
.BR localhost:6667 .
.TP
.B client
Outgoing endpoint.  Repeat up to 16 distinct endpoints.  Command-line
.B \-c
occurrences replace this entire list, preserving their order.
.TP
.B model_nick
Preferred model nick.  Default:
.BR agent0 .
.TP
.B operator_nick
Local operator nick.  Default: valid
.B USER
plus
.BR 0 ,
then a distinct
.B operator0
or
.BR localop0 .
.TP
.B room_name
Preferred room for current or future hosting.  Default: hostname.  Leading
.B #
is optional.
.TP
.B history_lines
Server and snapshot history, 1 through 1000.  Default: 200.
.PP
snajpagent peers negotiate incremental reconnect history using durable stream
IDs and event sequences.  Reopening the same server session preserves its IDs;
new server sessions have a new stream.  A client's saved cursor requests only
missing events.  Duplicate events are not appended, displayed or introduced as
new model input.  Current topic, membership and accepted nicknames still refresh.
An expired cursor reports a retention gap and replays the available newer tail.
New clients receive one bounded initial history.  Interrupted delivery is
recovered from the client's session until a model response consumes it.
.PP
Ordinary chat agent nicknames are blue; operator nicknames are cyan.  A mention
of a local operator or model highlights the timestamp, sender and message
separator in magenta, leaving plain and Markdown message bodies unchanged.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/config.ini
Default configuration.
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/auth/PROVIDER.json
Private credentials bound to provider, endpoint, and auth method.  Files must
be user-owned regular files with no group/other permissions.  Per-provider
locks coordinate refresh rotation and atomic replacement across processes.
Do not share or commit these files.  An alternate dotdir has independent logins.
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/models.json
Atomically replaced versioned provider/model registry containing advertised
token capacities and source-bound learned accounting.  Refresh only with
.BR /model\ cache .
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/prompt_history
Shared plaintext interactive prompt history.  It stores the newest 100 decoded
entries within 4 MiB as private mode 0600, one escaped physical line per
submission.  Another
.B --dotdir
selects another history file.
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/sessions/SESSION/events.jsonl
Append-only durable session log.
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/sessions/SESSION/lock
Live session lock.
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/trash/
Private deletion staging.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snajpagent/AGENTS.override.md
.TQ
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snajpagent/AGENTS.md
Optional global instructions.  If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, use
.IR $HOME/.config .
.PP
Project instructions are discovered from the repository root through the
workspace.  In each directory,
.B AGENTS.override.md
takes precedence over
.BR AGENTS.md .
Outside a repository, only the workspace directory is searched.  Global
instructions precede project instructions, followed by explicit
.B \-d
entry points.  The combined set is limited to 16 distinct canonical paths.
Files must be regular and non-symlink; discovery does not read their contents.
The model is given safely quoted paths and reads relevant guidance with normal
tools, subject to those tools' file and output limits.  An empty override still
takes precedence.  Per-turn metadata records advertised paths, not content
hashes or proof of reading.  This replaces the earlier pre-1.0 instruction
metadata format in place: sessions containing populated old instruction
metadata cannot be resumed by this build.  Their existing logs are not deleted.
.PP
Set
.B read_agents_md=false
under
.B [agent]
to disable both global and project discovery, but not explicit
.B \-d
roots.  The default working-document search location is the session workspace.
Useful notes retain findings, decisions, scoped corrections and unfinished work;
they are not a second goal controller.  Verify changeable facts when resuming,
keep proposals distinct from approval, and preserve local scope on each host.
No notes layout, recursive Markdown scan, or memory service is required.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Builds the default dotdir and fallback global-instruction path.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Selects the global-instruction directory.  It must be absolute.
.TP
.B EDITOR
Command used by
.BR /config .
.TP
.B USER
Supplies the default operator nick when valid and distinct.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables color in
.B auto
mode when present.
.TP
.B SSL_CERT_FILE
A nonempty value selects a CA file for provider, login and refresh HTTPS
connections, including HTTPS proxies.  It replaces the standalone build's
embedded CA data, or the native build's default CA file.  Certificate and
hostname verification remain enabled.  Keep this file trusted and current.
.TP
.B TERM
Enables terminal capabilities and color detection.
.TP
.BR LC_CTYPE , " LANG"
Must select a UTF-8 locale.
.TP
.B PATH
Used to resolve the executable path printed in a resume command.
.TP
.I "api_key = ${ENV_NAME}"
An explicit environment credential source must contain a nonempty value
of 1 through 16384 ASCII bytes without whitespace or controls.
.TP
.I "secret = ${ENV_NAME}"
Names an additional value hidden from tool children and redacted from output.
.SH AUTOMATION AND INTEGRATION
Use
.B \-e
for scripts.  It runs the supplied turn and any explicitly requested goal
continuations before exiting.  A prompt after
.B \-\-
preserves shell argument boundaries as spaces; a prompt from non-terminal stdin
preserves internal newlines and removes one final LF or CRLF.
.PP
Standard output carries one-shot model text.  Diagnostics and the resume hint
use standard error.  Redirected model text remains byte-exact and contains no
terminal color or Markdown presentation.
.PP
The normal coding-tool surface is
.BR exec_command ,
.BR write_stdin ,
and
.BR apply_patch .
Read-only queries instead expose the native functions
.BR list_files ,
.BR read_file ,
and
.BR grep .
They also expose provider-hosted
.BR web_search ,
executed by the provider, never as a local function or external command.
Listed AGENTS guidance remains subordinate to the read-only query and its
restrictions.  Other file and web contents are untrusted data, not instructions.
All other local tool dispatch, including process control, patches,
goal lifecycle, and IRC, is unavailable for that turn.
.PP
Native inspection accepts literal workspace-relative or absolute paths and
never follows symlinks, including parent components.  Listings include hidden
entries; special files and symlinks are labeled but not opened.  Reads require
regular UTF-8 files without NUL bytes.  Search uses POSIX extended regex or
explicit literal matching, with optional case folding, and reports skipped
non-text/special files.  It does not interpret ignore files or shell syntax.
This is not a confidentiality sandbox: local readable files are accessible.
Session/history persistence and filesystem access-time updates remain normal;
independently running processes are not stopped or made read-only.
.PP
Results are bounded to 256 KiB, lines to less than 64 KiB, each scan to 128 MiB,
directory entries to 100,000, and traversal depth to 32.  Listing/search use
offset/limit pagination (default 200, maximum 1,000 results); output identifies
incompleteness and the next offset.  Scan limits and I/O errors require a
narrower path.  Oversized file output fails explicitly: use smaller line ranges.
Directory order is sorted per directory; pagination observes the live tree,
not a filesystem snapshot.
.PP
Commands that yield, reach a foreground handoff deadline, or receive urgent
input continue under a process handle; the model can wait, interact, or
explicitly terminate them with
.BR write_stdin .
.PP
Process handles belong to the current foreground process, not a detached job
service.  Turn interruption, closure, or process exit closes managed commands;
resume does not revive an operating-system process.
.PP
Every
.B exec_command
and
.B write_stdin
call selects a positive
.B max_output_tokens
context bound or
.B null
for the configured
.BR "[tool] max_output_tokens" " ceiling (default: 6000)."
Larger requested bounds are capped at that ceiling; smaller bounds are honored.
The bound is persisted with the result and conservatively enforced as at most
one token per retained UTF-8 byte.  Truncated context retains bounded
provenance; complete redacted output remains in journal chunks.  Polls return
only newly collected output, with exact stream-range references.  Multiple
independent command calls may overlap; emission order does not establish a
dependency.  A batch shares the smallest positive yield interval.  Steering
hands back started commands and skips unstarted calls; it does not kill them.
Invalid or repeated same-handle interactions are not run.  All handles must
be settled before a final answer or goal completion.  Existing commands keep
their tool catalog; unrelated work does not require an immediate poll.
Hosted web search is a provider tool.  When the active provider URL has the
exact hostname
.BR openrouter.ai ,
snajpagent automatically uses
.BR openrouter:web_search ;
other providers retain
.BR web_search .
Hostname matching is case-insensitive and accepts an optional terminal DNS
dot and port, but not userinfo, subdomains, or lookalike hosts.  The provider
section name and model name do not control detection.  Use the usual
.B https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
base URL, model, and API credentials; no extra search setting, web plugin,
.B :online
suffix, or separate search credential is required.  This applies equally to
normal and read-only turns.  OpenRouter chooses the search engine by default;
search incurs provider charges in addition to model usage.
.PP
Networked mode adds
.BR irc_send ,
.BR irc_state ,
and privilege-checked
.BR irc_topic .
Sending and topic changes require a nullable string
.B destination
argument: a number from
.BR irc_state ,
.B all
for explicit broadcast, or
.B null
only for a sole destination.  Model routing is independent of the operator's
selection.  Results distinguish queue acceptance from failures, not remote receipt.
.PP
Explicit goal requests expose
.B create_goal
when no unfinished goal exists.  An active goal exposes
.B update_goal
for rewriting, completion, or blocking.  A paused or blocked goal exposes
neither.  Wording locks prevent model rewording, not completion or blocking.
.PP
IRC is the network integration.  The event log is durable internal state, not
a stable external API while snajpagent remains pre-1.0.
.SH IRC MODE
Listener mode creates one room.  Its default name is the hostname and its initial
topic is the server agent's absolute launch workspace.  snajpagent clients join
the advertised room automatically.
.PP
The local operator and ordinary IRC clients receive channel mode
.BR +o ;
the model nick does not.  Current channel operators may change the topic.
Only direct mentions of the model nick are urgent; ordinary messages from
local or channel operators remain background context.
Urgent input is coalesced and admitted at the earliest safe boundary;
generation reaches a safe stream boundary and commands are handed back alive.
.PP
Other messages, joins, leaves, mode changes, and topic changes enter model
context when convenient and do not force a reply.  First join and successful
compaction inject bounded room history.  The runtime reconnects outgoing links
without model babysitting.
.PP
Implicit outgoing nicks form zero-based sequences:
.BR agent0 ,
.BR agent1 ,
and so on, with the same rule for the operator's default.  When an explicit
preferred nick is already present, the client handles the server's normal
.B 433
reply by appending
.BR 1 ,
.BR 2 ,
and so on until registration succeeds.  The accepted nick is retained across
reconnects.
.PP
The terminal starts in the timestamped
.B chat
view.  Model text and tools belong to the
.B rollout
view regardless of verbosity.  Chat input goes to the selected room as the
local operator.  Multiple destinations receive short source/prompt labels;
a hosted room counts once regardless of how many clients joined it.
Explicit numbered sends and broadcasts also work from rollout, but ordinary
rollout input stays local:
while idle it starts a model turn, and during active work it steers that turn at
the next safe boundary.  It never emits an IRC message.  Incoming room traffic
is not automatically bridged between servers.  Model response text remains local;
.B irc_send
is the only model-authored path to a room message or notice.  If a
local-operator mention turn reaches a terminal response without a successful
.B irc_send
message, the model receives one reminder.  Notices do not satisfy that reply
requirement.  Other room traffic never forces a reply.
.PP
The accepted per-server nick drives mentions, echo suppression, and model
instructions.  Live IRC nick changes retain op status and produce room
notifications.  Chat uses the selected destination's accepted operator nick.
There is no local slash command for changing
nicks; standard IRC clients can use
.BR NICK .
.PP
Nicks are at most 30 bytes and local model/operator preferences must differ
under IRC case folding.  Room names are at most 50 bytes excluding
.BR # .
The server implements registration, join/part, messages/notices, topic,
names/who, nick and mode changes, liveness, and bounded history, not the entire
IRC protocol.  snajpagent clients expect its advertised single-room behavior.
.PP
The built-in IRC subset has no TLS or authentication.  A non-loopback listener
is suitable only for a trusted network or a separately secured tunnel.
.SH PROVIDER FAILURES
Transient transport, HTTP rate-limit/server, and structured Responses stream
failures receive at most two retries of the same request, with bounded backoff
and cancellation available during the wait.  Earlier turn/tool work is not
replayed.  Additional live chat, steering or queued input prevents further
retries, including during backoff.  Steering keeps its immediate handoff;
background chat and queued turns retain their normal admission rules, without
interrupting a healthy response.  When new input stops retrying a failed
response, armed queued turns proceed automatically, without an extra
.BR /next .
View/status controls alone do not veto retries.
Stream retries stop once output or tool activity has been observed;
malformed or ambiguous partial records are not automatically replayed.
.PP
Policy, access, quota and unknown structured failures are not transient retries.
For a pre-output
.B cyber_policy
rejection, the model instead receives up to three durable clarification
instructions per turn.  The original task remains intact: the instruction asks
for accurate restatement of ambiguous phrasing with purpose, actions, targets
and authorization unchanged, and operator clarification where needed.  New input
or observed output/activity prevents another automatic clarification.  A fourth
rejection ends the turn.  There is no provider switch or concealed task scope.
.PP
Rollout shows one concise "provider clarification N/3" notice at every verbosity
level.  Existing levels 4, 5 and 6 add event, redacted protocol and wire details.
Error codes, not message wording, determine eligibility.  A failed stream never
becomes a successful response merely because the connection closed.
.SH SESSION DURABILITY
Every accepted transition is appended and synced before it is treated as
durable.  Resume reconstructs turns, tool state, queue, goals, provider/model
selection, and admitted IRC state.
.PP
Normal exit prints a two-line resume hint for every resumable session.  It
includes the exact session, dotdir, explicit configuration, presentation
overrides, unused one-turn preferences, and effective IRC topology.  It never
includes prompts or credential values.  The command includes the effective
listener and outgoing list, or explicit
.B --no-listen
and
.B --no-client
for absent roles, so reused config defaults cannot resurrect removed endpoints.
Temporary connection outages do not remove desired roles from the command.
.PP
Resume preserves the saved goal state: active goals continue, while paused,
blocked, completed and cancelled goals keep their recorded states.  No goal
pause or resume transition is added merely by reopening the session.
Resume automatically displays the saved goal and wording,
including its status, revision, lock and blocker, even with history disabled.
Paused and blocked goal wording remains in ordinary model context after resume
and compaction; it need not be re-entered.  Inactive goals do not restart work.
Queued turns are also paused; use
.B /next
to restart FIFO execution.  Interrupted active turns are recovered from their
durable prefix.
.PP
History replay ends with a mode-style "history replayed" banner.  Replayed
chat timestamps have no per-message "history" label; stored historical flags
and model-input handling are unchanged.
.PP
SIGKILL, power loss, and fatal process corruption cannot guarantee cleanup or a
printed resume command.  Already synced events remain available.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGINT
Interactive Ctrl-C follows the composer rules above.  Outside that handler it
requests graceful shutdown.
.TP
.BR SIGHUP , " SIGTERM"
Request graceful shutdown, terminal restoration, IRC closure, and a resume
hint.  Exit status is 128 plus the signal number.
.TP
.B SIGTSTP
Ctrl-Z restores the terminal before suspension and redraws after continuation.
.TP
.B SIGPIPE
Ignored; write failures are handled as errors.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success or normal interactive exit.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line, configuration, locale, credential, prompt, or selection error.
.TP
.B 3
Durable state, local runtime, or IRC failure.
.TP
.B 4
One-shot provider, protocol, context, or tool-cycle failure.
.TP
.B 6
Terminal/output failure or one-shot interruption that cannot return to a prompt.
.TP
.B 128+N
Graceful exit after signal
.IR N .
.SH EXAMPLES
Start an interactive session in the current directory:
.PP
.nf
snajpagent
.fi
.PP
Execute a task from a script:
.PP
.nf
printf '%s\en' 'run the tests and summarize failures' | snajpagent -e
.fi
.PP
Resume the newest active session:
.PP
.nf
snajpagent --resume --last
.fi
.PP
Host a room and connect another agent:
.PP
.nf
snajpagent -s localhost:6667 -n builder -o alice -r work
snajpagent -c localhost:6667 -n reviewer -o bob
.fi
.SH SECURITY
The model and its tools act with the user's local authority.  Run in a suitable
workspace, inspect configuration, and protect the dotdir.  There is no command
approval sandbox.  snajpagent requires
its state directories to be mode 0700 and creates state files mode 0600.
.PP
Prompt history intentionally retains submitted operator text across sessions,
workspaces, and chat/rollout modes.  It cannot identify a secret deliberately
typed by the operator; remove or protect the history file with the same care as
shell history.  Session archive and deletion do not erase it.
.PP
Provider credential variables and configured
.B secret
sources are removed from tool environments when environment-backed, and resolved
values are redacted before tool output is
stored and displayed.  This does not make arbitrary workspace content safe to
send to a provider or publish.
.PP
Provider redirects are not followed.  The IRC server is unauthenticated and
unencrypted; keep its default local listener unless the surrounding network is
trusted or independently secured.
.PP
Verbosity 5 exposes prompts, source, tool output, and model content.  Review
terminal capture before sharing it.
